      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Prehistoric Native American peoples once inhabited the area that is now Barker Ten Mile, adapting to its changing environment over millennia.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/barker_ten_mile_north_carolina",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Because Robeson County is a minority‑majority county, Barker Ten Mile reflects a blend of Native American, European, African American, and Latinx cultures.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/barker_ten_mile_north_carolina",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Early land holdings stretched from present‑day Meadowbrook Cemetery north to Bee Gee Road and Meadow Road, marking the original family estates.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barker_Ten_Mile%2C_North_Carolina",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The community sits amid blackwater rivers and swamps, including Ten Mile Swamp and Saddle Tree Swamp, both culturally significant to the Lumbee.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11,
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/barker_ten_mile_north_carolina",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The Musselwhite and Mercer families have called Barker Ten Mile home since before the American Revolution, shaping its early history.",
